WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:09.224
- So welcome everybody to this
question and answer session

00:00:09.224 --> 00:00:12.477
about the documentary, "The
Neighborhood Storyteller".

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:16.140
You know, I just wanna take a moment

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:20.910
to say why we have this
extraordinary panel today.

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:25.470
Because today we have Dr. Rana Dajani,

00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:29.822
who is the founder of the
grassroot community-led program

00:00:29.822 --> 00:00:31.860
called We Love Reading,

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:35.430
which Asmaa talks about
in the documentary.

00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:40.430
We are also joined by the
protagonist in the documentary,

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:42.633
Asmaa Rashed,

00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:48.530
and the filmmaker Alejandra Alcala.

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:53.570
I'm going to ask my colleague, Dr. Dajani,

00:00:53.670 --> 00:00:57.180
to narrate for us the
beginning of this idea

00:00:57.180 --> 00:01:02.180
for the documentary and introduce
both Asmaa and Alejandra,

00:01:02.490 --> 00:01:06.720
and my role will simply be
at the moment to tell you

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:08.670
who I am and who Dr. Dajani is,

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:10.773
and why we're together here for you today.

00:01:11.790 --> 00:01:13.140
I am Catherine Panter-Brick.

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:16.380
I'm Professor of Anthropology,
Health, and Global Affairs

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:18.870
here at Yale University.

00:01:18.870 --> 00:01:23.219
Dr. Dajani is professor at
Hashemite University of Jordan.

00:01:23.219 --> 00:01:27.423
She is both a scholar and
a social entrepreneur.

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:29.910
As a scholar,

00:01:29.910 --> 00:01:32.954
Dr. Dajani and I have been
working over several years

00:01:32.954 --> 00:01:37.236
on understanding better
cohorts of Syrian refugee

00:01:37.236 --> 00:01:39.960
and Jordanian host communities

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:44.100
with respect to health
and social inclusion.

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:45.603
As a social entrepreneur,

00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:48.930
what you need to know
about Dr. Dajani Rana

00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:53.853
is that she's the founder
of We Love Reading,

00:01:55.295 --> 00:02:00.293
which now has established
more than 4,000 libraries

00:02:00.293 --> 00:02:03.450
in local communities in over 60 countries,

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:05.250
and the program that was imagined

00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:08.040
and undertaken in Jordan
in the first place.

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:08.910
And for this work,

00:02:08.910 --> 00:02:12.600
she's been recognized
and now with many awards.

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:13.440
She's been named

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:17.035
one of the most influential
women Arab scientists

00:02:17.035 --> 00:02:18.060
in the world.

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:21.660
But for this program, We Love Reading,

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:23.790
she's received in particular,

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:28.440
the 2021 Nansen UNHCR Refugee Award,

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:32.760
and has been named Social
Entrepreneur Award,

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:36.360
given the Social
Entrepreneur Award in 2022,

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:38.580
which is part of the World Economic Award

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:40.009
that selects social entrepreneurs

00:02:40.009 --> 00:02:43.043
and leaders around the world.

00:02:43.043 --> 00:02:46.830
And so, she has this
international recognition

00:02:46.830 --> 00:02:49.710
for something that she imagined

00:02:49.710 --> 00:02:51.550
and delivered and put together

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:55.920
at the start local community in Jordan.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:57.680
So Rana, thank you for being with us.

00:02:57.680 --> 00:02:59.640
Asmaa and Alejandra, thank
you for being with us as well

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:03.840
and please introduce us.

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:05.580
- Yes, thank you for inviting us

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:08.160
and holding this film screening.

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:09.510
Thank you everyone for being here,

00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:11.475
both online and in-person.

00:03:11.475 --> 00:03:13.315
So I'm gonna introduce the film

00:03:13.315 --> 00:03:16.470
and therefore introduce
Alejandra and Asmaa

00:03:16.470 --> 00:03:17.400
as part of the story,

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:18.690
'cause I think that's more entertaining.

00:03:18.690 --> 00:03:20.310
That's more fun.

00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:21.974
So actually We Love Reading,

00:03:21.974 --> 00:03:25.410
I started in 2006 reading
aloud in my neighborhood,

00:03:25.410 --> 00:03:27.120
just as you saw Asmaa was doing.

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:30.180
And then, I transformed
that into a training program

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:32.553
to train people to do
what I did in Jordan.

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:34.260
Fast forward,

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:36.033
the Syrian unrest happened

00:03:36.033 --> 00:03:37.771
and many Syrian refugees came to Jordan.

00:03:37.771 --> 00:03:40.920
Jordan is a haven for
refugees from Palestine.

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:43.140
I'm a daughter of refugees,

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:46.170
from Iraq, Syria, Yemen, even Ukraine.

00:03:46.170 --> 00:03:48.600
Today, we have refugees from Ukraine.

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:52.740
So I went to Zaatari camp
and I held a training there

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:54.450
and I trained Asmaa,

00:03:54.450 --> 00:03:55.514
and Asmaa took the training

00:03:55.514 --> 00:03:57.900
and as you see her life
was all transformed.

00:03:57.900 --> 00:04:00.720
So Asmaa, you know who Asmaa is.

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:02.416
She's a young woman, fascinating,

00:04:02.416 --> 00:04:05.820
amazing, full of energy
who's changing the world.

00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:07.230
Asmaa today is in France.

00:04:07.230 --> 00:04:08.240
She has been...

00:04:09.406 --> 00:04:12.230
As a refugee now, moved to France.

00:04:12.230 --> 00:04:14.122
You can guess what is
the first thing Asmaa did

00:04:14.122 --> 00:04:17.124
as soon as she set foot in Paris.

00:04:17.124 --> 00:04:18.960
- Eiffel tower?

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:21.593
- She gathered the children
and are reading aloud to them.

00:04:21.593 --> 00:04:23.247
She immediately called me and said,

00:04:23.247 --> 00:04:25.125
"I'm reading aloud to the kids."

00:04:25.125 --> 00:04:27.360
So Asmaa is a powerhouse.

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:30.000
Now, the Big Heart Foundation,

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:31.650
which is a foundation
based in the Emirates,

00:04:31.650 --> 00:04:33.406
had just started the whole
initiative of funding

00:04:33.406 --> 00:04:36.900
and supporting refugees in the region.

00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:40.020
And so, they contacted
the HOME Storytellers

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:43.050
of which Alejandra and
her father Francisco

00:04:43.050 --> 00:04:44.592
are the directors and producers

00:04:44.592 --> 00:04:46.440
and owners of this initiative,

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:47.273
of this organization.

00:04:47.273 --> 00:04:49.800
They're really well known
for many films they do,

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:51.630
documentary films.

00:04:51.630 --> 00:04:55.170
So Alejandra, who is a graphic designer,

00:04:55.170 --> 00:04:58.380
specialized in motion
graphics and post-production,

00:04:58.380 --> 00:04:59.213
and 3D design.

00:04:59.213 --> 00:05:00.180
She's fascinating.

00:05:00.180 --> 00:05:02.460
She's an amazing woman
as well, just had a baby.

00:05:02.460 --> 00:05:05.610
So her baby was with us on
camera a little while ago.

00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:06.994
So Alejandra and her father actually

00:05:06.994 --> 00:05:08.700
looked around in the region,

00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:11.880
where can they find a
real story of a woman,

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:13.680
who although as a refugee,

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:15.660
was able to make a difference in her life.

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:17.940
And so, they found We
Love Reading, our program,

00:05:17.940 --> 00:05:18.773
and they contacted us.

00:05:18.773 --> 00:05:20.850
And so, we introduced them to Asma

00:05:20.850 --> 00:05:24.360
and they spent all the
summer of 2021 filming Asma

00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:25.500
in Zaatari,

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:27.120
and the rest is history.

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:29.097
You saw the movie and the
movie has up till now,

00:05:29.097 --> 00:05:31.920
19 nominations, six awards.

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:33.690
Of them, the Toronto Film Award,

00:05:33.690 --> 00:05:35.640
the Mountain View Film Award,

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:37.672
and the Documentaries
Without Borders Award

00:05:37.672 --> 00:05:38.968
and it's just counting.

00:05:38.968 --> 00:05:40.440
Every day they call me up

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:41.430
and they say there's a new award.

00:05:41.430 --> 00:05:43.200
So we're really, really happy with that.

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:44.033
And there's many reasons why,

00:05:44.033 --> 00:05:46.590
and that's why we're gonna
have this panel discussion

00:05:46.590 --> 00:05:48.869
with Alejandra and Asmaa

00:05:48.869 --> 00:05:52.363
to tell us more about
their Asmaa's journey,

00:05:52.363 --> 00:05:54.240
Alejandra's journey,

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:55.857
and how they both are
mirrors for each other,

00:05:55.857 --> 00:05:57.480
Alejandra and Asmaa.

00:05:57.480 --> 00:05:59.400
They're very close in age

00:05:59.400 --> 00:06:02.081
and it's just fascinating
to hear them talk together.

00:06:02.081 --> 00:06:05.160
So welcome to Asmaa and Alejandra.

00:06:05.160 --> 00:06:08.497
I'll be translating for
Asmaa in Arabic both ways

00:06:08.497 --> 00:06:11.730
and we want people online
to post our questions

00:06:11.730 --> 00:06:12.563
in the Q and A.

00:06:12.563 --> 00:06:13.560
We will follow that.

00:06:13.560 --> 00:06:14.910
And of course,

00:06:14.910 --> 00:06:18.508
after we have a some Q and
A with Asmaa and Alejandra,

00:06:18.508 --> 00:06:20.400
we'll open it to the people here

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:22.474
so that you can ask
your questions as well.

00:06:22.474 --> 00:06:23.350
We're excited to learn.

00:06:23.350 --> 00:06:24.480
And by the way,

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:27.960
we created to go with the movie
a book, "My Mama's Magic",

00:06:27.960 --> 00:06:29.760
which is actually Asmaa's story,

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:31.965
in English and in Arabic.

00:06:31.965 --> 00:06:34.278
So you could take copies of these books

00:06:34.278 --> 00:06:37.020
and look at the children's
book that follows Asmaa

00:06:37.020 --> 00:06:38.310
in the eyes of her daughter.

00:06:38.310 --> 00:06:39.900
I mean you saw her daughter in the movie.

00:06:39.900 --> 00:06:43.410
So this children's book is
written from her daughter's voice

00:06:43.410 --> 00:06:45.540
and her vision of how she sees her mom

00:06:45.540 --> 00:06:48.140
and how her mother changed
and inspired her as well.

00:06:49.320 --> 00:06:51.750
Alright, so let's begin.

00:06:51.750 --> 00:06:54.300
Catherine, you're going to
lead us and talk about...

00:06:54.300 --> 00:06:56.220
As somebody who's been
involved in the research,

00:06:56.220 --> 00:06:58.920
because we've done a lot of
research among We Love Reading

00:06:58.920 --> 00:07:01.530
to really understand how the program

00:07:01.530 --> 00:07:04.020
has impacted Asmaa and millions of others,

00:07:04.020 --> 00:07:05.640
and how can we learn from that

00:07:05.640 --> 00:07:08.490
to inform other kinds of
program to do them right.

00:07:08.490 --> 00:07:10.950
We'll talk more about that as well.

00:07:10.950 --> 00:07:12.150
First, I think the...

00:07:12.150 --> 00:07:16.530
We'd like to talk to
Asmaa to start with her,

00:07:16.530 --> 00:07:20.640
the protagonist to share
with us her feelings

00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:21.750
about being...

00:07:21.750 --> 00:07:23.820
You know, she said in
the movie that she wanted

00:07:23.820 --> 00:07:25.140
to become famous and a writer

00:07:25.140 --> 00:07:26.645
and people who wanna
have pictures with her.

00:07:26.645 --> 00:07:28.644
Well, actually she got her wish

00:07:28.644 --> 00:07:30.150
so we'll ask her about that.

00:07:30.150 --> 00:07:31.680
I think we'll kick off with that question

00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:33.088
and then we'll turn to Alejandra.

00:07:33.088 --> 00:07:36.338
(Rana speaking Arabic)

00:07:49.243 --> 00:07:52.576
(Asmaa speaking Arabic)

00:08:05.610 --> 00:08:08.557
- So Asmaa is saying that
yes, her dream came true

00:08:08.557 --> 00:08:09.390
and this is...

00:08:09.390 --> 00:08:12.270
Her point is that she
wants all the young girls

00:08:12.270 --> 00:08:13.740
to believe in their dreams

00:08:13.740 --> 00:08:15.779
and that yes dreams can come true.

00:08:15.779 --> 00:08:17.730
All they need to do is just dream

00:08:17.730 --> 00:08:19.244
and think big and trust themselves

00:08:19.244 --> 00:08:21.243
and have confidence in themselves.

00:08:22.860 --> 00:08:24.360
Alejandra over to you.

00:08:24.360 --> 00:08:26.280
So I'd love you to share

00:08:26.280 --> 00:08:29.400
how you first encountered We Love Reading

00:08:29.400 --> 00:08:33.036
and then Asmaa and how
that really inspired you

00:08:33.036 --> 00:08:34.923
to create this movie.

00:08:36.180 --> 00:08:37.530
- Hi everyone.

00:08:37.530 --> 00:08:39.070
Rana, great introduction

00:08:40.311 --> 00:08:42.630
and it's so great to be here.

00:08:42.630 --> 00:08:43.923
I really feel honored.

00:08:45.060 --> 00:08:48.594
So actually this was like
a really unique opportunity

00:08:48.594 --> 00:08:51.810
because when the Big Heart
Foundation approached us,

00:08:51.810 --> 00:08:53.884
they gave us like a white paper.

00:08:53.884 --> 00:08:57.254
They did a brief that was very open

00:08:57.254 --> 00:08:59.370
and it gave us the freedom to be able

00:08:59.370 --> 00:09:02.190
to find something that as filmmakers

00:09:02.190 --> 00:09:04.050
would inspire us as well.

00:09:04.050 --> 00:09:06.540
So that was a really
interesting opportunity

00:09:06.540 --> 00:09:08.040
and we did a lot of research.

00:09:08.040 --> 00:09:11.130
We found a lot of amazing
organizations out there.

00:09:11.130 --> 00:09:14.253
But I think what rang a
bell with We Love Reading,

00:09:15.570 --> 00:09:16.530
I think it's...

00:09:16.530 --> 00:09:18.040
First of all, it's uniqueness

00:09:19.140 --> 00:09:22.680
and something that was
really like beautiful

00:09:22.680 --> 00:09:24.390
about the magic of reading.

00:09:24.390 --> 00:09:25.223
And we thought,

00:09:25.223 --> 00:09:27.270
"Oh, there could be like something..."

00:09:27.270 --> 00:09:28.765
In the end, it's also art.

00:09:28.765 --> 00:09:33.083
We thought that it could be
very interesting for the film,

00:09:33.083 --> 00:09:35.160
and the more we got involved in reading

00:09:35.160 --> 00:09:37.350
about what We Love Reading does,

00:09:37.350 --> 00:09:38.595
we realized that...

00:09:38.595 --> 00:09:41.340
And after obviously
conversations with Rana,

00:09:41.340 --> 00:09:45.300
we realized about that reading is a bridge

00:09:45.300 --> 00:09:47.070
for something bigger

00:09:47.070 --> 00:09:50.640
and that was the most amazing thing.

00:09:50.640 --> 00:09:53.070
And when Rana told us
it's a two day training,

00:09:53.070 --> 00:09:54.927
and when we heard about Asmaa's story

00:09:54.927 --> 00:09:57.360
and how she continued for years and years

00:09:57.360 --> 00:10:01.590
and the life transformation
that not only brought to her

00:10:01.590 --> 00:10:03.723
but it also brought to her community,

00:10:05.790 --> 00:10:07.627
I mean that was probably
the moment that we said,

00:10:07.627 --> 00:10:08.638
"Oh my God, this organization,

00:10:08.638 --> 00:10:11.520
we have to collaborate with them."

00:10:11.520 --> 00:10:15.780
And then, I think it was us
that suggested meeting Asmaa

00:10:15.780 --> 00:10:18.423
because we found an article
on your website Rana.

00:10:19.260 --> 00:10:21.240
And so, we got introduced to Asmaa

00:10:21.240 --> 00:10:24.188
and I think from the three first seconds

00:10:24.188 --> 00:10:25.237
of our conversation,

00:10:25.237 --> 00:10:27.450
we fell in love with her.

00:10:27.450 --> 00:10:30.420
Because what you see on
screen is the real Asmaa

00:10:30.420 --> 00:10:33.120
and it's her laugh, her eyes,

00:10:33.120 --> 00:10:37.830
the way she expresses and
her desire to not only...

00:10:37.830 --> 00:10:39.750
It goes so much further,

00:10:39.750 --> 00:10:43.020
her wish to make a change in the world

00:10:43.020 --> 00:10:45.933
and that is what captivated us.

00:10:47.490 --> 00:10:48.480
Hearing her story

00:10:48.480 --> 00:10:51.900
and seeing that if we were
able to make this into a film

00:10:51.900 --> 00:10:53.940
that we could share with the world,

00:10:53.940 --> 00:10:57.180
imagine how much larger her voice could be

00:10:57.180 --> 00:10:59.454
and how much larger her impact could be

00:10:59.454 --> 00:11:01.410
in the lives of many,

00:11:01.410 --> 00:11:03.963
especially women and girls
in situations like her.

00:11:06.480 --> 00:11:07.313
- Oh, thank you.

00:11:07.313 --> 00:11:08.146
Thank you Alejandra

00:11:08.146 --> 00:11:10.079
for sharing how this whole thing started.

00:11:10.079 --> 00:11:11.250
That's amazing.

00:11:11.250 --> 00:11:12.994
Catherine, do you wanna please?

00:11:12.994 --> 00:11:14.025
No, please continue.

00:11:14.025 --> 00:11:18.300
I mean, I guess Asmaa has
to participate in Arabic.

00:11:18.300 --> 00:11:20.657
So yeah, so I'll just explain.

00:11:20.657 --> 00:11:23.907
(Rana speaking Arabic)

00:11:39.780 --> 00:11:43.113
(Asmaa speaking Arabic)

00:12:03.510 --> 00:12:07.200
So I asked Asmaa how was her reaction

00:12:07.200 --> 00:12:08.340
when she knew that they were gonna come

00:12:08.340 --> 00:12:09.450
and make a movie about her?

00:12:09.450 --> 00:12:10.352
So she said,

00:12:10.352 --> 00:12:11.790
"Well first of all,

00:12:11.790 --> 00:12:13.770
there was a lot of phone
calls going back and forth,

00:12:13.770 --> 00:12:14.850
introducing them to each other.

00:12:14.850 --> 00:12:15.810
Because Alejandra,

00:12:15.810 --> 00:12:17.940
we went through a lot of
loopholes to get them permission

00:12:17.940 --> 00:12:20.147
to go into Zaatari with all the cameras

00:12:20.147 --> 00:12:22.110
and all the equipment.

00:12:22.110 --> 00:12:25.890
It was a COVID time and it was Ramadhan,

00:12:25.890 --> 00:12:27.150
and there was limited time

00:12:27.150 --> 00:12:28.800
and they were on a right?

00:12:28.800 --> 00:12:29.633
And there was like,

00:12:29.633 --> 00:12:31.590
they had to finish within
a certain period of time

00:12:31.590 --> 00:12:32.694
but we were able to do it all.

00:12:32.694 --> 00:12:34.920
Asmaa says that she became

00:12:34.920 --> 00:12:36.660
very close friends with Alejandra,

00:12:36.660 --> 00:12:37.493
which they are.

00:12:37.493 --> 00:12:38.326
They talk all the time,

00:12:38.326 --> 00:12:39.300
especially after this movie.

00:12:39.300 --> 00:12:42.540
They're being called every
couple of weeks for a screaming

00:12:42.540 --> 00:12:44.250
or to give a talk or something.

00:12:44.250 --> 00:12:45.810
And so Asmaa said,

00:12:45.810 --> 00:12:47.580
it also made her get to know more people

00:12:47.580 --> 00:12:49.245
and other people, the film crew,

00:12:49.245 --> 00:12:52.890
to think of a different
perspective of life.

00:12:52.890 --> 00:12:53.820
Alright, we have another question.

00:12:53.820 --> 00:12:55.140
Catherine, please.

00:12:55.140 --> 00:12:55.973
- A question for Alejandra.

00:12:55.973 --> 00:12:59.370
I know that people might be curious to see

00:12:59.370 --> 00:13:01.383
what it takes to do a
documentary like this.

00:13:01.383 --> 00:13:04.110
You have obviously, you know,

00:13:04.110 --> 00:13:08.250
you are obviously portraying
young girls talking to you

00:13:08.250 --> 00:13:11.160
about the aspirations and
it's very moving in that way.

00:13:11.160 --> 00:13:13.530
They say very precious,
interesting things like,

00:13:13.530 --> 00:13:16.650
I want my life to matter
and to be somebody.

00:13:16.650 --> 00:13:17.913
But what is the,

00:13:19.320 --> 00:13:22.719
like Rana just mentioned many cameras,

00:13:22.719 --> 00:13:24.420
to have to get permission to Zaatari.

00:13:24.420 --> 00:13:26.730
Can you tell us a little
bit about what it takes

00:13:26.730 --> 00:13:29.148
to do a film like this from
the technical point of view

00:13:29.148 --> 00:13:31.710
and from the organizational point of view,

00:13:31.710 --> 00:13:34.290
not just the inspiration?

00:13:34.290 --> 00:13:36.008
- Yes, I can of course.

00:13:36.008 --> 00:13:38.370
I just wanted to add a little bit of,

00:13:38.370 --> 00:13:42.210
a tiny backend of the first question.

00:13:42.210 --> 00:13:45.210
That I will never forget
what Rana said to us

00:13:45.210 --> 00:13:47.363
when we first spoke about
the magic that happens

00:13:47.363 --> 00:13:49.590
after the training program.

00:13:49.590 --> 00:13:53.070
I think that was kind of
a thing that triggered us

00:13:53.070 --> 00:13:54.450
to make this documentary

00:13:54.450 --> 00:13:57.050
because Asmaa was not only repeating

00:13:57.050 --> 00:13:59.310
what she had learned
in the training course

00:13:59.310 --> 00:14:02.021
that she did for many, many years to come,

00:14:02.021 --> 00:14:03.753
but she had gotten to a point

00:14:03.753 --> 00:14:07.800
where she had taken the skills
learned at We Love of Reading

00:14:07.800 --> 00:14:11.340
and transformed them into an initiative

00:14:11.340 --> 00:14:13.031
that came from her heart.

00:14:13.031 --> 00:14:18.031
That's why she was trying to
gather girls that had lived

00:14:18.600 --> 00:14:22.410
or were facing a situation
that she had lived in her past

00:14:22.410 --> 00:14:25.413
and that had left a scar
that she needed to cure.

00:14:26.460 --> 00:14:28.557
So I just wanted to add that part.

00:14:28.557 --> 00:14:32.760
And then, regarding the
how we did it technically

00:14:32.760 --> 00:14:34.350
and all the cameras,

00:14:34.350 --> 00:14:36.390
it was a lot, a lot of work

00:14:36.390 --> 00:14:39.693
and a lot of persistence
and a lot of patience.

00:14:41.701 --> 00:14:42.534
On one side,

00:14:42.534 --> 00:14:46.403
the technical part and the
logistics was very difficult

00:14:46.403 --> 00:14:49.023
because of course it's
in a foreign language.

00:14:50.010 --> 00:14:52.050
It's in a country that...

00:14:52.050 --> 00:14:53.910
I mean, I had never worked there before

00:14:53.910 --> 00:14:57.260
so you don't really know
how the bureaucracy is,

00:14:57.260 --> 00:14:59.369
how you have to find the permits.

00:14:59.369 --> 00:15:02.190
So we were lucky enough to be able to work

00:15:02.190 --> 00:15:05.370
with the Royal Film Commission in Jordan,

00:15:05.370 --> 00:15:10.370
who was really helpful
while getting the permits.

00:15:10.800 --> 00:15:13.620
We also had Guffran from
the We Love Reading team

00:15:13.620 --> 00:15:15.783
who was our local fixer,

00:15:16.740 --> 00:15:19.230
which is a very good
friend of Asmaa as well.

00:15:19.230 --> 00:15:20.063
And funny enough,

00:15:20.063 --> 00:15:22.680
all three of us are
practically the same age.

00:15:22.680 --> 00:15:26.730
So we all became really
good, good, good friends.

00:15:26.730 --> 00:15:29.760
And so, it was really difficult

00:15:29.760 --> 00:15:31.710
and I have to add another challenge

00:15:31.710 --> 00:15:33.900
was that we were facing COVID.

00:15:33.900 --> 00:15:37.830
So each time that we were able to finally,

00:15:37.830 --> 00:15:40.140
it seemed that the permits
were gonna come out.

00:15:40.140 --> 00:15:43.350
There was some sort of delay in the travel

00:15:43.350 --> 00:15:44.520
because of COVID

00:15:44.520 --> 00:15:46.053
and we had to re-

00:15:48.948 --> 00:15:50.100
I forgot the...

00:15:50.100 --> 00:15:51.600
- Schedule, reschedule.

00:15:51.600 --> 00:15:54.750
- Yeah, like reschedule
and remake the permits.

00:15:54.750 --> 00:15:56.898
And so, maybe some of our team members

00:15:56.898 --> 00:15:58.754
had other prior commitments

00:15:58.754 --> 00:16:01.140
and they couldn't work with us anymore,

00:16:01.140 --> 00:16:02.523
so we had to find backups.

00:16:02.523 --> 00:16:06.690
It was an ongoing process
for months and months.

00:16:06.690 --> 00:16:08.940
Even when we arrived in Jordan,

00:16:08.940 --> 00:16:11.850
it seemed we had all the permits
for all our team members,

00:16:11.850 --> 00:16:15.630
and unfortunately our
director of photography

00:16:15.630 --> 00:16:18.540
did not have a permit to enter Zaatari.

00:16:18.540 --> 00:16:22.641
So from 20 days of filming,
it was reduced to 14 days.

00:16:22.641 --> 00:16:26.010
And the time that we had applied for

00:16:26.010 --> 00:16:28.740
was to have the entire day at Zaatari

00:16:28.740 --> 00:16:32.070
and it was reduced to leaving
at three in the afternoon,

00:16:32.070 --> 00:16:34.202
and we were followed by a policeman

00:16:34.202 --> 00:16:37.950
who was like forcing us
to leave at 3:00 PM Sharp.

00:16:37.950 --> 00:16:40.710
So we had to work around those challenges.

00:16:40.710 --> 00:16:42.300
And as documentary filmmakers,

00:16:42.300 --> 00:16:43.519
these are kind of the surprises

00:16:43.519 --> 00:16:46.230
that you find along the way.

00:16:46.230 --> 00:16:49.020
That's the technical part.

00:16:49.020 --> 00:16:54.020
The story part and being
able to not make people panic

00:16:55.770 --> 00:16:57.570
when you have thousands of cameras there,

00:16:57.570 --> 00:16:58.403
which we didn't.

00:16:58.403 --> 00:16:59.490
We are a very small team

00:16:59.490 --> 00:17:01.641
and that's something that
we take care of a lot

00:17:01.641 --> 00:17:04.830
because we know it can be intimidating.

00:17:04.830 --> 00:17:06.720
But part of our process

00:17:06.720 --> 00:17:09.480
and what's really important
to us and a priority

00:17:09.480 --> 00:17:13.234
is being able to gain
the trust of the people

00:17:13.234 --> 00:17:17.640
who are sharing their
story with us to film.

00:17:17.640 --> 00:17:18.960
Because if you don't have trust,

00:17:18.960 --> 00:17:20.430
you don't have anything.

00:17:20.430 --> 00:17:23.580
And so, that was something
that for months prior

00:17:23.580 --> 00:17:28.580
to the filmmaking itself,
Asmaa and I exchanged letters.

00:17:29.940 --> 00:17:31.260
We exchanged phone calls.

00:17:31.260 --> 00:17:32.422
I asked her to keep a diary.

00:17:32.422 --> 00:17:36.450
I shared with her intimate
things about my life as well

00:17:36.450 --> 00:17:38.530
because it's a two-way relationship.

00:17:38.530 --> 00:17:40.645
It doesn't work one way.

00:17:40.645 --> 00:17:42.750
I think that really helped

00:17:42.750 --> 00:17:45.210
for when I arrived at Asma's door

00:17:45.210 --> 00:17:48.273
to be able to not arrive
as a stranger anymore.

00:17:49.770 --> 00:17:50.940
Asmaa I mean,

00:17:50.940 --> 00:17:55.290
I can't even say like how
amazing she is in person,

00:17:55.290 --> 00:17:58.579
in front of a camera, behind
the camera, at all times.

00:17:58.579 --> 00:18:01.380
So that was amazing to see.

00:18:01.380 --> 00:18:03.097
And with the girls,

00:18:03.097 --> 00:18:06.790
I think the first days they
were a little bit scared

00:18:07.940 --> 00:18:10.680
and surprised,

00:18:10.680 --> 00:18:12.210
but I think it was very important

00:18:12.210 --> 00:18:14.850
that they saw that Asmaa trusted us.

00:18:14.850 --> 00:18:17.253
And if they trust Asmaa,
they trust us as well.

00:18:18.240 --> 00:18:20.010
So that was really helpful.

00:18:20.010 --> 00:18:21.860
And again, we're a small, small team

00:18:21.860 --> 00:18:26.580
and so we don't wanna make
too much noise in the room.

00:18:26.580 --> 00:18:27.813
That helps as well.

00:18:29.370 --> 00:18:30.840
- Thank you.

00:18:30.840 --> 00:18:32.160
Yeah, I would like to...

00:18:32.160 --> 00:18:35.370
I think also being a woman as well

00:18:35.370 --> 00:18:36.203
- Oh yeah.

00:18:36.203 --> 00:18:37.620
- That makes a huge difference

00:18:37.620 --> 00:18:40.320
for that trust and that relatedness,

00:18:40.320 --> 00:18:41.910
and I'll never forget when you said

00:18:41.910 --> 00:18:44.010
that you were pregnant
at that time, right?

00:18:44.010 --> 00:18:46.110
- No, I got pregnant after editing.

00:18:46.110 --> 00:18:49.320
- Oh well, I mean when you
were doing all the editing...

00:18:49.320 --> 00:18:50.153
- [Alejandra] Oh yeah.

00:18:50.153 --> 00:18:51.540
- And you said that...

00:18:51.540 --> 00:18:54.060
You just realized that you
and Asmaa were the same age,

00:18:54.060 --> 00:18:55.530
and you were having your first baby

00:18:55.530 --> 00:18:57.900
and just relating those human things

00:18:57.900 --> 00:18:59.310
that were all the same in the end, right?

00:18:59.310 --> 00:19:01.650
That we're all humans
and we share different...

00:19:01.650 --> 00:19:03.030
There's similar experiences,

00:19:03.030 --> 00:19:04.170
although in different contexts

00:19:04.170 --> 00:19:05.963
and that shared humanity is something

00:19:05.963 --> 00:19:08.580
that we need to keep
reminding ourselves of

00:19:08.580 --> 00:19:10.830
and which she showed in the movie.

00:19:10.830 --> 00:19:11.663
- Yeah.

00:19:11.663 --> 00:19:14.130
- Asmaa, I would like to ask you,

00:19:14.130 --> 00:19:17.081
I'll ask it in English and
then I'll translate in Arabic.

00:19:17.081 --> 00:19:20.760
I would like you to share how,

00:19:20.760 --> 00:19:23.070
what was the process of change within you

00:19:23.070 --> 00:19:24.810
after you took the training

00:19:24.810 --> 00:19:29.109
and how did that inspire you

00:19:29.109 --> 00:19:32.211
or change you to go out
and do another project,

00:19:32.211 --> 00:19:34.860
which is the project that
was actually later on

00:19:34.860 --> 00:19:36.150
filmed with the teenager groups,

00:19:36.150 --> 00:19:38.401
because We Love Reading is
with the younger children.

00:19:38.401 --> 00:19:41.234
(speaking Arabic)

00:20:02.224 --> 00:20:05.641
(Asmaa speaking Arabic)

00:21:45.810 --> 00:21:48.390
When she started reading
aloud to the children,

00:21:48.390 --> 00:21:50.490
this led to an openness about her.

00:21:50.490 --> 00:21:53.520
She had just had a miscarriage
and she was feeling depressed

00:21:53.520 --> 00:21:56.430
and not feeling well,
like very optimistic.

00:21:56.430 --> 00:21:59.280
And so through the reading
aloud to the children,

00:21:59.280 --> 00:22:01.263
it inspired her to have a love of life.

00:22:02.130 --> 00:22:04.110
Psychologically, she became more positive.

00:22:04.110 --> 00:22:07.050
She had a reason to live.

00:22:07.050 --> 00:22:08.160
She was more present.

00:22:08.160 --> 00:22:10.710
I'm trying to translate
verbatim to what she said.

00:22:10.710 --> 00:22:12.480
That the relationship with the children

00:22:12.480 --> 00:22:13.755
she was reading aloud to,

00:22:13.755 --> 00:22:15.540
inspired her passion

00:22:15.540 --> 00:22:18.150
and give her a sense of belonging

00:22:18.150 --> 00:22:20.520
and that's when she
realized that some of...

00:22:20.520 --> 00:22:23.820
Because again, she started
reading aloud in 2014.

00:22:23.820 --> 00:22:24.679
So after a couple of years,

00:22:24.679 --> 00:22:27.660
some of the girls who
used to come grew up.

00:22:27.660 --> 00:22:29.940
They became 13, 14 years old.

00:22:29.940 --> 00:22:30.990
And so, they stopped coming

00:22:30.990 --> 00:22:33.570
because they thought this
is just for little children

00:22:33.570 --> 00:22:34.800
and that's when she,

00:22:34.800 --> 00:22:37.110
because of that feeling of like passion

00:22:37.110 --> 00:22:38.881
and openness and reality...

00:22:38.881 --> 00:22:41.010
I am here, I exist.

00:22:41.010 --> 00:22:42.450
And of course, she went
through other things.

00:22:42.450 --> 00:22:43.963
She became a writer
for the local magazine.

00:22:43.963 --> 00:22:46.050
She wrote a children's book.

00:22:46.050 --> 00:22:49.260
She became a teacher in the
local school in Zaatari.

00:22:49.260 --> 00:22:54.090
So all these little things
inspired her that...

00:22:54.090 --> 00:22:55.140
She identified a gap,

00:22:55.140 --> 00:22:57.210
and the gap was that these teenager girls.

00:22:57.210 --> 00:23:01.080
They weren't coming to the read aloud.

00:23:01.080 --> 00:23:02.700
So she had to create something for them

00:23:02.700 --> 00:23:05.100
and that inspired her to
start her program Let's Read

00:23:05.100 --> 00:23:06.600
that you saw in the movie

00:23:06.600 --> 00:23:08.520
where she started gathering
the teenager girls,

00:23:08.520 --> 00:23:10.380
and she also designed it

00:23:10.380 --> 00:23:12.570
to cater to the needs of these teenagers.

00:23:12.570 --> 00:23:15.240
So she chose books that
were self-help books,

00:23:15.240 --> 00:23:16.650
skill-based books,

00:23:16.650 --> 00:23:18.000
so that they would learn these skills

00:23:18.000 --> 00:23:19.590
whether they got married or not.

00:23:19.590 --> 00:23:20.917
But it was their decision to think,

00:23:20.917 --> 00:23:22.793
"Okay, what can I do with my life?"

00:23:22.793 --> 00:23:25.290
She wanted subtly inspiring them to go

00:23:25.290 --> 00:23:26.663
and get your education.

00:23:26.663 --> 00:23:28.530
You don't have to
necessarily follow the path

00:23:28.530 --> 00:23:30.940
that your community is
prescribing for you,

00:23:30.940 --> 00:23:32.370
but you can think on your own.

00:23:32.370 --> 00:23:35.520
And you saw that through the
testimonials of the teenagers,

00:23:35.520 --> 00:23:36.840
as they were rethinking

00:23:36.840 --> 00:23:39.450
how they see themselves in this world

00:23:39.450 --> 00:23:40.800
and in their own community.

00:23:42.302 --> 00:23:44.190
You have a question, Catherine?

00:23:44.190 --> 00:23:45.960
Oh, Asmaa.

00:23:45.960 --> 00:23:49.530
It was a hard question.

00:23:49.530 --> 00:23:53.010
I wanted to say how impressive it was

00:23:53.010 --> 00:23:56.340
that you gave this message
of personal change,

00:23:56.340 --> 00:23:58.230
and I wanted to ask you

00:23:58.230 --> 00:24:02.290
whether you also felt
that there might be or not

00:24:02.290 --> 00:24:04.750
some intergenerational change.

00:24:04.750 --> 00:24:06.513
So how does that affect,

00:24:09.780 --> 00:24:11.070
maybe your own children?

00:24:11.070 --> 00:24:13.083
Now of course you are in Paris,

00:24:13.920 --> 00:24:17.040
they see the world and you see
the world in a different way.

00:24:17.040 --> 00:24:19.170
But I wanted you perhaps
to comment on anything

00:24:19.170 --> 00:24:22.866
about whether that sense of personal worth

00:24:22.866 --> 00:24:26.970
and purpose and work
and service had changed,

00:24:26.970 --> 00:24:28.470
not only you as an individual,

00:24:28.470 --> 00:24:31.983
but your community around
you and the next generation?

00:24:32.919 --> 00:24:36.169
(Rana speaking Arabic)

00:25:19.162 --> 00:25:22.495
(Asmaa speaking Arabic)

00:26:28.480 --> 00:26:29.313
- Wow.

00:26:29.313 --> 00:26:30.603
Yeah, I wish you understood Arabic.

00:26:31.710 --> 00:26:33.930
So Asmaa says,

00:26:33.930 --> 00:26:36.540
of course impact was not only on her,

00:26:36.540 --> 00:26:39.012
but on her children and her community.

00:26:39.012 --> 00:26:41.463
This positive feeling,

00:26:42.900 --> 00:26:43.733
how she changed,

00:26:43.733 --> 00:26:46.410
and how she changed her
perspective affected them

00:26:46.410 --> 00:26:48.900
but she elaborates a lot
more about going to France.

00:26:48.900 --> 00:26:50.940
So she said when she went to France,

00:26:50.940 --> 00:26:54.300
that her positive energy was transcendent.

00:26:54.300 --> 00:26:55.950
It was very contagious,

00:26:55.950 --> 00:26:56.970
and she says this is something

00:26:56.970 --> 00:26:58.260
that she learned from reading.

00:26:58.260 --> 00:26:59.543
Because when you read,

00:26:59.543 --> 00:27:00.960
you learn about other people.

00:27:00.960 --> 00:27:02.130
You become more empathetic.

00:27:02.130 --> 00:27:03.086
So everybody was telling her,

00:27:03.086 --> 00:27:05.250
"Don't you have a cultural
shock when you went to France?"

00:27:05.250 --> 00:27:06.420
She said, "No, I don't."

00:27:06.420 --> 00:27:08.855
And she says, it's all
because she reads a lot.

00:27:08.855 --> 00:27:11.550
She's also inspired
through We Love Reading,

00:27:11.550 --> 00:27:12.960
as we always say,

00:27:12.960 --> 00:27:15.000
is not just to read,
to learn about others,

00:27:15.000 --> 00:27:16.380
but also to tell your story,

00:27:16.380 --> 00:27:18.570
to have that confidence
about telling your story.

00:27:18.570 --> 00:27:21.660
So she tells her story and
she tells it to inspire others

00:27:21.660 --> 00:27:24.120
and to share her positive
energy with others,

00:27:24.120 --> 00:27:25.080
and those are her words

00:27:25.080 --> 00:27:26.820
and that's what she does in France

00:27:26.820 --> 00:27:29.403
and inspires all the people around her.

00:27:31.290 --> 00:27:34.470
I'm gonna add something just
in the spirit of all this,

00:27:34.470 --> 00:27:37.640
which to me was something that I...

00:27:37.640 --> 00:27:39.900
It was a reflection so to speak.

00:27:39.900 --> 00:27:41.667
You watched the documentary

00:27:41.667 --> 00:27:44.580
and you saw it was actually Asmaa's story.

00:27:44.580 --> 00:27:46.770
It's not the story of We Love Reading.

00:27:46.770 --> 00:27:49.366
It's not my story who
founded We Love Reading.

00:27:49.366 --> 00:27:50.880
It's Asma's story,

00:27:50.880 --> 00:27:53.970
and inherent in that is a big, big lesson

00:27:53.970 --> 00:27:55.168
is that we didn't want...

00:27:55.168 --> 00:27:56.970
It's not the story of We Love Reading

00:27:56.970 --> 00:27:59.250
because if it's the
story of an organization,

00:27:59.250 --> 00:28:02.190
then we're removing the
humanity from the people

00:28:02.190 --> 00:28:05.190
that we are trying to work together with.

00:28:05.190 --> 00:28:06.930
This is very different.

00:28:06.930 --> 00:28:07.920
This is unique.

00:28:07.920 --> 00:28:09.270
This is very special,

00:28:09.270 --> 00:28:11.130
and it was a collective
effort of all of us

00:28:11.130 --> 00:28:14.010
to make sure that We Love
Reading was subtly there

00:28:14.010 --> 00:28:15.510
and that it was the...

00:28:15.510 --> 00:28:17.640
We Love Reading did not empower us.

00:28:17.640 --> 00:28:18.840
Asmaa's empowered.

00:28:18.840 --> 00:28:20.850
She has it in her, like every one of us.

00:28:20.850 --> 00:28:23.093
We all had, it's how we
evolved as a species,

00:28:23.093 --> 00:28:24.540
as humans.

00:28:24.540 --> 00:28:26.550
It's just that we need a
little catalyst, right?

00:28:26.550 --> 00:28:29.790
An activation energy to rekindle that fire

00:28:29.790 --> 00:28:31.593
that for different reasons may have,

00:28:32.914 --> 00:28:35.152
needs a little bit of rekindling.

00:28:35.152 --> 00:28:36.420
We Love Reading just does that.

00:28:36.420 --> 00:28:38.220
It just nurtures it, rekindles it.

00:28:38.220 --> 00:28:39.630
But like, it's her story.

00:28:39.630 --> 00:28:40.890
And that's why We Love Reading,

00:28:40.890 --> 00:28:43.470
it's actually every person's
story and they own it.

00:28:43.470 --> 00:28:47.722
It's that we call it our secret
sauce or the magic, right?

00:28:47.722 --> 00:28:51.540
It's that that agency that
they feel I can do anything

00:28:51.540 --> 00:28:53.190
and then they go around and do everything

00:28:53.190 --> 00:28:54.023
and anything around them,

00:28:54.023 --> 00:28:57.205
and Asmaa is the epitome example of this.

00:28:57.205 --> 00:28:59.610
I think that's a lesson for,

00:28:59.610 --> 00:29:02.070
for program development
and program interventions

00:29:02.070 --> 00:29:03.180
and international NGOs,

00:29:03.180 --> 00:29:05.610
as we develop programs to serve,

00:29:05.610 --> 00:29:09.990
to support using humanitarian context

00:29:09.990 --> 00:29:11.340
or in development.

00:29:11.340 --> 00:29:15.758
How do we develop using that
strategy and that approach?

00:29:15.758 --> 00:29:18.210
Because then it's sustainable.

00:29:18.210 --> 00:29:19.860
Asmaa carries the program wherever she is.

00:29:19.860 --> 00:29:21.603
You don't have to do anything.

00:29:22.961 --> 00:29:24.270
It'll be taken up

00:29:24.270 --> 00:29:26.310
because it belongs to the
person who actually did it

00:29:26.310 --> 00:29:27.450
because Asma did it.

00:29:27.450 --> 00:29:28.530
No one else.

00:29:28.530 --> 00:29:31.829
And of course nobody, this is
not about money either right?

00:29:31.829 --> 00:29:34.290
Asma did it because she
cares about her community.

00:29:34.290 --> 00:29:35.640
She cares, she belongs,

00:29:35.640 --> 00:29:37.892
and she talks about this
feeling of belonging

00:29:37.892 --> 00:29:40.290
to my community, to my society,

00:29:40.290 --> 00:29:42.210
and that's why I do it because I care.

00:29:42.210 --> 00:29:43.860
I read a lot to the kids because I care.

00:29:43.860 --> 00:29:45.496
I identify challenges in my community

00:29:45.496 --> 00:29:48.390
and come up with solutions because I care.

00:29:48.390 --> 00:29:49.467
I think that trust,

00:29:49.467 --> 00:29:52.230
which Alejandra talked about
and Asmaa talked about,

00:29:52.230 --> 00:29:55.290
that trust that we need to
foster between us as human beings

00:29:55.290 --> 00:29:56.123
is so important.

00:29:56.123 --> 00:29:56.956
Sometimes we forget it.

00:29:56.956 --> 00:29:59.687
I think it's really, really important.

00:29:59.687 --> 00:30:01.500
So I just wanted to touch on those

00:30:01.500 --> 00:30:05.940
because I think that's a huge
learning lesson for all of us

00:30:05.940 --> 00:30:06.773
and to me.

00:30:06.773 --> 00:30:09.120
I keep reminding on myself of that.

00:30:09.120 --> 00:30:09.953
So I think,

00:30:09.953 --> 00:30:10.980
do you have any questions

00:30:10.980 --> 00:30:12.810
or shall we open it to the audience?

00:30:12.810 --> 00:30:14.070
- We should open the magic.

00:30:14.070 --> 00:30:16.350
- Please, yes.

00:30:16.350 --> 00:30:20.850
So who would like to ask,
start or comment or critique?

00:30:20.850 --> 00:30:22.500
By the way, I love critique.

00:30:22.500 --> 00:30:23.333
Because when you critique,

00:30:23.333 --> 00:30:24.166
that's how we learn.

00:30:24.166 --> 00:30:25.080
If everybody's gonna
say, "Oh, this is great,"

00:30:25.080 --> 00:30:26.130
we're never gonna learn.

00:30:26.130 --> 00:30:27.693
So please bring it on.

00:30:29.160 --> 00:30:33.220
- [Participant] How did
Alejandra and Asmaa communicate?

00:30:33.220 --> 00:30:34.110
- Great question.

00:30:34.110 --> 00:30:35.670
Alejandra?

00:30:35.670 --> 00:30:37.170
- I dunno if everybody can hear.

00:30:37.170 --> 00:30:38.003
- Can you hear that?

00:30:38.003 --> 00:30:39.270
Did you hear that Alejandra?

00:30:39.270 --> 00:30:42.848
- The question is how Alejandra
and Asmaa communicated.

00:30:42.848 --> 00:30:43.898
(Alejandra laughing)

00:30:43.898 --> 00:30:47.613
- It's funny, you always
find ways to communicate.

00:30:48.450 --> 00:30:50.472
If there's a need, there's a way.

00:30:50.472 --> 00:30:52.702
And in the...

00:30:52.702 --> 00:30:54.453
Via virtually, it's easier

00:30:54.453 --> 00:30:57.870
because I downloaded this translator

00:30:57.870 --> 00:31:00.510
that automatically translates my WhatsApp.

00:31:00.510 --> 00:31:03.475
So I was talking to Arabic...

00:31:03.475 --> 00:31:06.783
Sorry, in Arabic to
Asmaa through WhatsApp.

00:31:08.766 --> 00:31:10.860
Funny enough, after we created the film,

00:31:10.860 --> 00:31:13.308
now she talks English to me via WhatsApp.

00:31:13.308 --> 00:31:16.260
So she's translating now,

00:31:16.260 --> 00:31:18.810
and then we exchanged letters.

00:31:18.810 --> 00:31:20.580
So she would share them in Arabic,

00:31:20.580 --> 00:31:22.140
I would Google translate them

00:31:22.140 --> 00:31:23.850
and then I would share mine in English.

00:31:23.850 --> 00:31:25.386
She would Google translate.

00:31:25.386 --> 00:31:30.386
And for the film, we actually
hired a spontaneous...

00:31:31.110 --> 00:31:33.453
Sorry, a simultaneous translator.

00:31:34.440 --> 00:31:36.819
So she, her name's Amani.

00:31:36.819 --> 00:31:38.660
She did a great job, by the way.

00:31:38.660 --> 00:31:40.920
I don't know who has that capacity

00:31:40.920 --> 00:31:44.460
to like be hearing
something in one language

00:31:44.460 --> 00:31:46.414
and translating it into another one.

00:31:46.414 --> 00:31:50.402
She was in a different room
from where we were filming,

00:31:50.402 --> 00:31:52.997
connected to the microphone
that we were using.

00:31:52.997 --> 00:31:56.940
So she would hear Asmaa talking,

00:31:56.940 --> 00:31:59.610
she would then translate into a recorder

00:31:59.610 --> 00:32:01.920
that was connected into my headphones

00:32:01.920 --> 00:32:05.220
and to the director of
photography's headphones.

00:32:05.220 --> 00:32:09.420
And so, we were hearing the
English as she was talking,

00:32:09.420 --> 00:32:13.290
and the recorder that
Amani was talking to.

00:32:13.290 --> 00:32:16.258
we used afterwards for
the editing process.

00:32:16.258 --> 00:32:19.080
So we used one channel that was in English

00:32:19.080 --> 00:32:20.850
and then one channel that was in Arabic.

00:32:20.850 --> 00:32:23.195
So I could use one ear for one language

00:32:23.195 --> 00:32:24.810
and the other for the other one.

00:32:24.810 --> 00:32:29.040
And so, that helped us make this basic cut

00:32:29.040 --> 00:32:31.800
from all the footage that we had.

00:32:31.800 --> 00:32:32.702
Then eventually,

00:32:32.702 --> 00:32:36.390
with a guide of what
the story was gonna be,

00:32:36.390 --> 00:32:37.920
how the story was gonna be told,

00:32:37.920 --> 00:32:41.490
we then sent out to translate to a company

00:32:41.490 --> 00:32:43.473
that actually hires refugees.

00:32:44.430 --> 00:32:46.994
So we wanted to also be able

00:32:46.994 --> 00:32:51.720
to continue supporting
refugee self-reliance

00:32:51.720 --> 00:32:52.863
in that way as well.

00:32:54.090 --> 00:32:56.403
And everything that we didn't understand,

00:32:57.240 --> 00:32:58.290
we actually...

00:32:58.290 --> 00:33:00.630
I think that played in our favor

00:33:00.630 --> 00:33:05.630
because you start to
focus more on the emotions

00:33:05.970 --> 00:33:10.020
and the human behaviors
and how someone is acting.

00:33:10.020 --> 00:33:14.640
I think that was captured
in Asmaa's project

00:33:14.640 --> 00:33:15.750
with the girls.

00:33:15.750 --> 00:33:18.150
If you can, you can tell
that during the entire film.

00:33:18.150 --> 00:33:20.970
We focused on the
expressions on the faces.

00:33:20.970 --> 00:33:22.710
So many times when we
weren't hearing Amani,

00:33:22.710 --> 00:33:24.390
because it was hard for her to translate

00:33:24.390 --> 00:33:25.530
when the girls were laughing

00:33:25.530 --> 00:33:28.050
and talking all at the same time.

00:33:28.050 --> 00:33:29.736
We were just signaling like this girl,

00:33:29.736 --> 00:33:31.830
this girl, this girl, her face,

00:33:31.830 --> 00:33:33.420
and it was really emotional

00:33:33.420 --> 00:33:35.270
and I think that played in our favor.

00:33:37.890 --> 00:33:39.180
- Absolutely.

00:33:39.180 --> 00:33:40.036
Another question.

00:33:40.036 --> 00:33:40.869
Yes, please.

00:33:40.869 --> 00:33:43.215
- [Participant] The girls in the movie,

00:33:43.215 --> 00:33:47.048
did you select them or
are they the whole set?

00:33:48.092 --> 00:33:49.590
- So Alejandra, can you hear that?

00:33:49.590 --> 00:33:50.423
- [Alejandra] Yes, yes.

00:33:50.423 --> 00:33:52.216
- Yeah, please go ahead.

00:33:52.216 --> 00:33:57.216
- No, we didn't want to intervene
at all in the storyline.

00:33:57.600 --> 00:33:59.850
We left Asmaa that job.

00:33:59.850 --> 00:34:01.876
She selected, she created the project,

00:34:01.876 --> 00:34:04.170
she designed everything.

00:34:04.170 --> 00:34:05.220
She selected the girls.

00:34:05.220 --> 00:34:06.510
We actually didn't know the girls

00:34:06.510 --> 00:34:07.650
until we were actually there

00:34:07.650 --> 00:34:09.210
when Asmaa introduced us.

00:34:09.210 --> 00:34:11.850
She actually got the
permits from the families

00:34:11.850 --> 00:34:14.797
to be able to have the
consent for filmmaking.

00:34:14.797 --> 00:34:17.610
She solved anything regarding the project.

00:34:17.610 --> 00:34:19.470
It was her, all her.

00:34:19.470 --> 00:34:20.662
- So maybe I'll ask her
the question, right?

00:34:20.662 --> 00:34:21.495
- [Alejandra] Yeah.

00:34:21.495 --> 00:34:24.745
(Rana speaking Arabic)

00:34:31.308 --> 00:34:34.641
(Asmaa speaking Arabic)

00:35:07.145 --> 00:35:09.450
- So when she selected
the girls for her program,

00:35:09.450 --> 00:35:11.113
'cause again she's not doing
this for the movie right?

00:35:11.113 --> 00:35:13.860
This is what she's actually doing.

00:35:13.860 --> 00:35:14.850
So the girls she selected,

00:35:14.850 --> 00:35:15.683
she went to make sure

00:35:15.683 --> 00:35:17.610
that they came from diverse backgrounds

00:35:17.610 --> 00:35:18.690
so they could learn from each other

00:35:18.690 --> 00:35:19.590
and share from each other.

00:35:19.590 --> 00:35:20.970
So some were in school,

00:35:20.970 --> 00:35:21.930
some were out of school,

00:35:21.930 --> 00:35:24.361
remember this was in the
middle of COVID right?

00:35:24.361 --> 00:35:27.090
And she also invited girls,

00:35:27.090 --> 00:35:28.710
one of the girls was actually engaged,

00:35:28.710 --> 00:35:29.790
almost to be married.

00:35:29.790 --> 00:35:31.560
She wanted her to also
be part of the program

00:35:31.560 --> 00:35:34.283
so that she could learn what
other opportunities she had,

00:35:34.283 --> 00:35:35.820
even if she was married.

00:35:35.820 --> 00:35:37.230
That she can still do things

00:35:37.230 --> 00:35:40.770
and it's in her hand if she
pursues the path of reading

00:35:40.770 --> 00:35:41.603
and education.

00:35:41.603 --> 00:35:42.704
Education, it's not just the school right?

00:35:42.704 --> 00:35:44.970
We can educate ourselves
in different forms

00:35:44.970 --> 00:35:46.070
and in different ways.

00:35:46.980 --> 00:35:49.290
And actually one of the young girls,

00:35:49.290 --> 00:35:50.601
I don't know if...

00:35:50.601 --> 00:35:51.720
Well you wouldn't know,

00:35:51.720 --> 00:35:52.639
I know them all.

00:35:52.639 --> 00:35:54.480
(indistinct) she was actually,

00:35:54.480 --> 00:35:56.340
before this Asmaa had trained...

00:35:56.340 --> 00:35:57.847
She came to Asmaa and asked her,

00:35:57.847 --> 00:35:59.090
"Could you train me how to read aloud

00:35:59.090 --> 00:36:00.960
so I can start reading
aloud to the children?"

00:36:00.960 --> 00:36:02.370
So when she was a little bit older,

00:36:02.370 --> 00:36:04.345
she started gathering the kids
and reading aloud to them.

00:36:04.345 --> 00:36:07.854
And then, she became part
of Asmaa's program herself.

00:36:07.854 --> 00:36:11.104
(Rana speaking Arabic)

00:36:13.181 --> 00:36:14.880
Any other question?

00:36:14.880 --> 00:36:16.083
Yes, James.

00:36:18.060 --> 00:36:19.140
- [James] It's really a pleasure.

00:36:19.140 --> 00:36:21.930
I guess I found myself wondering,

00:36:21.930 --> 00:36:26.930
have you been able to interact
and hear from the fathers

00:36:27.150 --> 00:36:30.555
and what kind of perspective they have?

00:36:30.555 --> 00:36:32.153
I guess it's really fascinating to think

00:36:32.153 --> 00:36:37.153
about watching the movie and
sort of being interconnected

00:36:37.290 --> 00:36:38.970
and I guess I'm just curious about

00:36:38.970 --> 00:36:41.610
sort of where they stand in this reality,

00:36:41.610 --> 00:36:44.073
but thank you so much.

00:36:44.073 --> 00:36:45.543
It's really inspiring.

00:36:46.530 --> 00:36:47.637
- Thank you, thank you.

00:36:47.637 --> 00:36:49.124
Well, this is for Asmaa.

00:36:49.124 --> 00:36:52.374
(Rana speaking Arabic)

00:37:05.675 --> 00:37:09.008
(Asmaa speaking Arabic)

00:38:01.860 --> 00:38:04.890
So she's saying in terms
of her own personal story,

00:38:04.890 --> 00:38:06.780
she was always very close to her father.

00:38:06.780 --> 00:38:07.680
And when she was young,

00:38:07.680 --> 00:38:09.359
she would go to his
library and read his books.

00:38:09.359 --> 00:38:12.000
Although they were much, I mean high...

00:38:12.000 --> 00:38:16.786
Well, above her level of reading, right?

00:38:16.786 --> 00:38:21.579
Most of them would be more very
theory or above her stand...

00:38:21.579 --> 00:38:23.370
Well, I don't wanna use the word standard,

00:38:23.370 --> 00:38:24.203
but above her level...

00:38:24.203 --> 00:38:25.937
- Understanding?
- Understanding, yes, yes.

00:38:25.937 --> 00:38:28.170
Now, in terms of her project,

00:38:28.170 --> 00:38:32.040
she wanted to build that
relationship between the girls,

00:38:32.040 --> 00:38:33.987
the teenager girls and their fathers

00:38:33.987 --> 00:38:36.780
in the community that she's part of.

00:38:36.780 --> 00:38:38.190
Girls are closer to their mothers.

00:38:38.190 --> 00:38:40.470
I think all over girls are
always closer to the mother.

00:38:40.470 --> 00:38:42.202
That's just how things are.

00:38:42.202 --> 00:38:44.204
So she wanted to close that gap

00:38:44.204 --> 00:38:47.220
and strengthen the relationship.

00:38:47.220 --> 00:38:48.240
So when she went,

00:38:48.240 --> 00:38:49.073
as you saw in the movie,

00:38:49.073 --> 00:38:51.270
she would talk to the
father about her project,

00:38:51.270 --> 00:38:52.650
explaining what it's all about,

00:38:52.650 --> 00:38:55.830
to gain his trust and
understanding of what it's about.

00:38:55.830 --> 00:38:57.861
She would encourage the
girls to go back home

00:38:57.861 --> 00:39:00.750
and talk about the program
with their fathers, right?

00:39:00.750 --> 00:39:03.330
So they would share and create a dialogue,

00:39:03.330 --> 00:39:06.630
an exchange between the
father and the daughters

00:39:06.630 --> 00:39:09.093
so to strengthen that relationship.

00:39:10.170 --> 00:39:11.003
Maybe...

00:39:11.003 --> 00:39:13.962
(Rana speaking Arabic)

00:39:18.081 --> 00:39:21.414
(Asmaa speaking Arabic)

00:40:35.435 --> 00:40:36.268
- Yeah, okay.

00:40:36.268 --> 00:40:38.700
So Asmaa,

00:40:38.700 --> 00:40:40.622
she follows up and talks about how...

00:40:40.622 --> 00:40:42.360
I followed up with a question

00:40:42.360 --> 00:40:46.140
about what was the feedback
from the fathers, right?

00:40:46.140 --> 00:40:47.310
To follow up on your question.

00:40:47.310 --> 00:40:50.010
She said for the fathers,

00:40:50.010 --> 00:40:52.651
it was difficult at first to agree

00:40:52.651 --> 00:40:54.173
for the girls to go outside their home

00:40:54.173 --> 00:40:55.501
and participate in activities.

00:40:55.501 --> 00:40:56.340
And she said,

00:40:56.340 --> 00:40:59.100
this is understandable because
they're coming out of a war

00:40:59.100 --> 00:41:01.650
and that has its own trauma

00:41:01.650 --> 00:41:03.235
of being protective about your family,

00:41:03.235 --> 00:41:05.115
protective about your children.

00:41:05.115 --> 00:41:08.280
But eventually, they
would comply and change.

00:41:08.280 --> 00:41:10.080
She said opposite the mothers,

00:41:10.080 --> 00:41:11.587
they were more compliant
and they would say,

00:41:11.587 --> 00:41:14.220
"Yes, take them and teach them,

00:41:14.220 --> 00:41:15.639
but you have to make sure that
we convinced the dad, right?"

00:41:15.639 --> 00:41:18.465
So she had to convince the fathers

00:41:18.465 --> 00:41:20.880
so that they would allow
their daughters to come.

00:41:20.880 --> 00:41:22.650
And then, I followed up with
a question about her husband

00:41:22.650 --> 00:41:24.780
because we saw her
husband in the movie Fadi,

00:41:24.780 --> 00:41:25.700
who was very supportive

00:41:25.700 --> 00:41:27.240
in how they play rolled in the beginning.

00:41:27.240 --> 00:41:29.130
If you noticed, they
were play rolling, right?

00:41:29.130 --> 00:41:31.590
Clocking and he was
giving her a hard time,

00:41:31.590 --> 00:41:32.790
telling her that's what
you're gonna expect

00:41:32.790 --> 00:41:34.080
from these fathers.

00:41:34.080 --> 00:41:35.730
And she said at the
beginning, her husband,

00:41:35.730 --> 00:41:36.780
he's part of the community

00:41:36.780 --> 00:41:40.680
and these are the expectations
of the different rules

00:41:40.680 --> 00:41:42.720
of women within that community

00:41:42.720 --> 00:41:45.600
and taking into
consideration the war, right?

00:41:45.600 --> 00:41:47.040
Because that changes.

00:41:47.040 --> 00:41:50.250
I don't know how many of
us have experienced war.

00:41:50.250 --> 00:41:51.089
It changes.

00:41:51.089 --> 00:41:53.340
It changes your values
and not your values,

00:41:53.340 --> 00:41:55.517
but what are your fears, right?

00:41:55.517 --> 00:41:58.200
Where you're scared and
you wanna be protective.

00:41:58.200 --> 00:41:59.100
So she said at the beginning,

00:41:59.100 --> 00:42:03.090
her husband was not very
supportive of her activities

00:42:03.090 --> 00:42:04.140
in doing things.

00:42:04.140 --> 00:42:05.910
But she said, it's my role,

00:42:05.910 --> 00:42:10.500
her role and the role of
woman to believe in themselves

00:42:10.500 --> 00:42:12.900
and to have the confidence and persistence

00:42:12.900 --> 00:42:15.180
to show that this is my passion.

00:42:15.180 --> 00:42:16.380
I believe in this.

00:42:16.380 --> 00:42:17.790
I want this.

00:42:17.790 --> 00:42:18.623
It's good.

00:42:18.623 --> 00:42:19.456
It's good for me.

00:42:19.456 --> 00:42:20.400
It's good for my community.

00:42:20.400 --> 00:42:24.087
And it was her persistence
and her passion that,

00:42:24.087 --> 00:42:25.830
and the work that she did on the ground

00:42:25.830 --> 00:42:26.868
that proved to her husband

00:42:26.868 --> 00:42:29.340
that yes this is making her happier.

00:42:29.340 --> 00:42:31.080
It's making her a better person.

00:42:31.080 --> 00:42:32.040
She's actually doing things,

00:42:32.040 --> 00:42:33.240
making a difference in the community.

00:42:33.240 --> 00:42:36.240
So he became her staunch
supporter in many, many things.

00:42:36.240 --> 00:42:38.190
And more than she's expressing,

00:42:38.190 --> 00:42:40.320
we know intricately of
things that she faced

00:42:40.320 --> 00:42:42.420
and her husband was the staunch supporter,

00:42:42.420 --> 00:42:43.253
in supporting her,

00:42:43.253 --> 00:42:45.693
being her back as she continued

00:42:45.693 --> 00:42:47.973
in the different activities
in her community.

00:42:48.990 --> 00:42:52.582
And maybe I wanna touch on this

00:42:52.582 --> 00:42:54.450
and invite Catherine,

00:42:54.450 --> 00:42:56.250
because we're doing
research on this right?

00:42:56.250 --> 00:42:58.920
Trying to understand the relationships

00:42:58.920 --> 00:43:01.620
between the mothers and the
fathers and this touches on it,

00:43:01.620 --> 00:43:04.497
and more broadly on what is
the impact of We Love Reading

00:43:04.497 --> 00:43:06.096
on wellbeing and happiness

00:43:06.096 --> 00:43:08.850
and what is the mediator of that right?

00:43:08.850 --> 00:43:10.200
Is it the agency?

00:43:10.200 --> 00:43:12.540
Is it the motivation to lead?

00:43:12.540 --> 00:43:14.430
What are those, the social networks?

00:43:14.430 --> 00:43:15.930
So we've been working on this

00:43:15.930 --> 00:43:17.460
and I'd love to hear from you.

00:43:17.460 --> 00:43:21.690
Oh, hmm.

00:43:21.690 --> 00:43:22.950
- That's a...

00:43:22.950 --> 00:43:24.030
Well quickly on,

00:43:24.030 --> 00:43:29.030
you mean on the program and
on the feelings of women.

00:43:29.310 --> 00:43:31.380
Yeah, so I think...

00:43:31.380 --> 00:43:33.480
I think one of the surprising things,

00:43:33.480 --> 00:43:35.910
our research touches
many different facets,

00:43:35.910 --> 00:43:39.630
but one of the things that
we're trying to understand

00:43:39.630 --> 00:43:42.210
when people use the word
agency and empowerment.

00:43:42.210 --> 00:43:43.043
And she says,

00:43:43.043 --> 00:43:45.330
I have tried to change from within

00:43:45.330 --> 00:43:48.870
how people understand
that process of change

00:43:48.870 --> 00:43:50.698
and how it relates to therefore

00:43:50.698 --> 00:43:54.960
not just your personal
psychological life satisfaction

00:43:54.960 --> 00:43:55.793
and wellbeing,

00:43:55.793 --> 00:44:00.720
but also how that changes
your ties with the community,

00:44:00.720 --> 00:44:02.340
which is about networks,

00:44:02.340 --> 00:44:05.910
and how those ties change the
opportunities that you have

00:44:05.910 --> 00:44:09.810
to learn, work, and thrive.

00:44:09.810 --> 00:44:12.317
I think that's the idea.

00:44:12.317 --> 00:44:15.659
We're testing that with groups of women,

00:44:15.659 --> 00:44:20.659
Syrian and Jordanian, who are
or not taking this program.

00:44:21.810 --> 00:44:22.643
We Love Reading.

00:44:22.643 --> 00:44:24.690
So it's a randomized controlled trial.

00:44:24.690 --> 00:44:27.097
We do this to have the
best scientific evidence

00:44:27.097 --> 00:44:32.097
of what such a program as
an exemplar program can show

00:44:32.174 --> 00:44:34.290
with outcomes on the individual,

00:44:34.290 --> 00:44:39.290
on society, on the learning
capacities of children.

00:44:40.530 --> 00:44:43.380
Basically, if I were a poet,

00:44:43.380 --> 00:44:45.480
I would say on the body,
the mind, and the brain,

00:44:45.480 --> 00:44:46.950
and the soul.

00:44:46.950 --> 00:44:50.490
So that is little, little pieces

00:44:50.490 --> 00:44:53.010
to understand the value of such program.

00:44:53.010 --> 00:44:54.810
But if I may say so too,

00:44:54.810 --> 00:44:57.060
and I think you, Alejandra
you touched on that,

00:44:57.060 --> 00:44:57.960
which was extraordinary.

00:44:57.960 --> 00:45:01.890
It's a two day non-paid program

00:45:01.890 --> 00:45:06.890
of basically of enabling people

00:45:06.930 --> 00:45:09.930
to make changes in the
place that they are,

00:45:09.930 --> 00:45:12.300
and so it's a very simple idea.

00:45:12.300 --> 00:45:16.334
That's why it's can be
exported to different places.

00:45:16.334 --> 00:45:18.750
It's a very grounded idea.

00:45:18.750 --> 00:45:21.103
So kudos to you Rana for finding something

00:45:21.103 --> 00:45:24.750
that is grown out of
your learning experience

00:45:24.750 --> 00:45:27.000
and speaks to that
experience of other people.

00:45:27.000 --> 00:45:27.833
Yeah.

00:45:27.833 --> 00:45:28.920
- And kudos to all those women

00:45:28.920 --> 00:45:30.240
and who are actually doing it,

00:45:30.240 --> 00:45:31.245
but thank you for sharing that.

00:45:31.245 --> 00:45:32.253
I wanna say...

00:45:32.253 --> 00:45:33.720
- It's not just women who are doing it.

00:45:33.720 --> 00:45:34.553
- Oh, yes.

00:45:34.553 --> 00:45:36.477
- And so, that's important too.

00:45:36.477 --> 00:45:39.178
It's young people and men.

00:45:39.178 --> 00:45:40.645
And so Rana,

00:45:40.645 --> 00:45:45.180
also told me one day it was
about changing mindsets.

00:45:45.180 --> 00:45:46.260
And to change mindsets,

00:45:46.260 --> 00:45:49.800
you have to identify the
changemakers in your communities.

00:45:49.800 --> 00:45:51.750
And I was say, "Oh,
mindsets, changemakers."

00:45:51.750 --> 00:45:53.070
I remember those words.

00:45:53.070 --> 00:45:55.470
But the changemakers do not
necessarily need to be women,

00:45:55.470 --> 00:45:56.303
they can be men.

00:45:56.303 --> 00:45:57.136
They can be youth.

00:45:57.136 --> 00:45:58.770
They can be anyone.

00:45:58.770 --> 00:45:59.700
- Absolutely.

00:45:59.700 --> 00:46:00.750
And actually touching on that,

00:46:00.750 --> 00:46:02.520
I wanna emphasize another point

00:46:02.520 --> 00:46:05.640
is that We Love Reading
is a program developed

00:46:05.640 --> 00:46:07.470
within the community by the community.

00:46:07.470 --> 00:46:09.150
I belong to that community.

00:46:09.150 --> 00:46:11.220
I'm hlaf Syrian, half Palestinian.

00:46:11.220 --> 00:46:12.517
I'm not somebody who came abroad and said,

00:46:12.517 --> 00:46:13.860
"I wanna develop a program."

00:46:13.860 --> 00:46:14.940
So I'm from the community

00:46:14.940 --> 00:46:16.650
and I think that...

00:46:16.650 --> 00:46:18.900
Inherent in that is the success.

00:46:18.900 --> 00:46:21.150
It's because when you
develop for yourself,

00:46:21.150 --> 00:46:23.340
you know better where that
challenge is coming from

00:46:23.340 --> 00:46:25.860
and you know better to
develop a solution that works,

00:46:25.860 --> 00:46:28.680
that's practical, that
doesn't burn you out.

00:46:28.680 --> 00:46:30.214
And to me,

00:46:30.214 --> 00:46:32.299
for three years as I was
developing the program,

00:46:32.299 --> 00:46:34.140
meaning trying it myself,

00:46:34.140 --> 00:46:34.973
trial and error,

00:46:34.973 --> 00:46:36.030
trial and error in my neighborhood,

00:46:36.030 --> 00:46:37.470
reading aloud to the children.

00:46:37.470 --> 00:46:39.005
I feel it was...

00:46:39.005 --> 00:46:41.590
People call it now human-centered design

00:46:41.590 --> 00:46:43.344
and what Asmaa's doing
with her teenagers, right?

00:46:43.344 --> 00:46:46.980
She's developing a program
with the girls as she moves.

00:46:46.980 --> 00:46:49.470
Well, I liken it to evolution.

00:46:49.470 --> 00:46:50.700
I'm a biologist, right?

00:46:50.700 --> 00:46:52.043
So it's that natural selection.

00:46:52.043 --> 00:46:54.030
You're trial and error, trial and error,

00:46:54.030 --> 00:46:55.440
until you get it to the most...

00:46:55.440 --> 00:46:57.660
You reduce it to the
most empirical formula

00:46:57.660 --> 00:47:00.840
where with the least input
you get the maximum output,

00:47:00.840 --> 00:47:02.370
and that's what we are reading is about.

00:47:02.370 --> 00:47:03.581
And the reason,

00:47:03.581 --> 00:47:04.900
I mean people talk about scaling.

00:47:04.900 --> 00:47:07.181
I hate the word scaling, right?

00:47:07.181 --> 00:47:08.014
Why?

00:47:08.014 --> 00:47:11.910
Because it's implies that I have something

00:47:11.910 --> 00:47:14.145
and I know better than you, right?

00:47:14.145 --> 00:47:16.110
That's not what We Love Reading about.

00:47:16.110 --> 00:47:17.286
And so what you said...

00:47:17.286 --> 00:47:19.200
- I think you used the word diffusion.

00:47:19.200 --> 00:47:20.286
- Diffusion or yeah,

00:47:20.286 --> 00:47:21.900
it's like a movement.

00:47:21.900 --> 00:47:23.370
People pick it up, right?

00:47:23.370 --> 00:47:26.738
So that's why our logo
is changing mindsets

00:47:26.738 --> 00:47:29.010
through reading to create a changemaker.

00:47:29.010 --> 00:47:31.290
So we're using reading as that catalyst

00:47:31.290 --> 00:47:33.540
for people to unlock
their inner potential,

00:47:33.540 --> 00:47:34.680
realizing if I can do this,

00:47:34.680 --> 00:47:35.880
what else can I do?

00:47:35.880 --> 00:47:37.890
So it's really changing
the mindsets of people

00:47:37.890 --> 00:47:38.723
around the world.

00:47:38.723 --> 00:47:42.090
So every person becomes a change
maker within her community.

00:47:42.090 --> 00:47:45.180
And so, it's changing the wording I think.

00:47:45.180 --> 00:47:47.130
This is part of systems
change in the world.

00:47:47.130 --> 00:47:49.110
I mean we have these paradigm shifts

00:47:49.110 --> 00:47:50.430
that people are talking about.

00:47:50.430 --> 00:47:51.840
How do you change the system?

00:47:51.840 --> 00:47:53.700
So it changes by how you design,

00:47:53.700 --> 00:47:54.630
the terms you use,

00:47:54.630 --> 00:47:57.450
the definitions that we use to,

00:47:57.450 --> 00:48:01.401
to make that shift in the
framework of how things are done.

00:48:01.401 --> 00:48:03.774
So Rana is a social entrepreneur.

00:48:03.774 --> 00:48:05.010
You can...

00:48:05.010 --> 00:48:06.630
She has the idea

00:48:06.630 --> 00:48:09.510
and the perseverance

00:48:09.510 --> 00:48:11.640
and the passion to see that.

00:48:11.640 --> 00:48:13.290
We are partnering because of research.

00:48:13.290 --> 00:48:15.630
But I just want to say
Alejandra for instance,

00:48:15.630 --> 00:48:16.860
you're very integral in this

00:48:16.860 --> 00:48:21.240
because you are the person
who partners with Asmaa

00:48:22.860 --> 00:48:25.388
and with Rana for example to show.

00:48:25.388 --> 00:48:28.500
You show and tell the story

00:48:28.500 --> 00:48:31.440
and that's a really remarkable
partnership I think.

00:48:31.440 --> 00:48:35.399
And so, thank you for
making that documentary

00:48:35.399 --> 00:48:37.560
because otherwise without you,

00:48:37.560 --> 00:48:41.900
it doesn't have the
coverage that it deserves.

00:48:41.900 --> 00:48:42.733
- Absolutely.
- Yeah.

00:48:42.733 --> 00:48:46.890
I think that as reading is the catalyst,

00:48:46.890 --> 00:48:49.357
film is also a catalyst for the same thing

00:48:49.357 --> 00:48:54.357
to diffuse a message that
should be hopefully brought

00:48:54.600 --> 00:48:57.240
to inspire many, many, many more lives.

00:48:57.240 --> 00:49:00.494
I think I just wanted to add
regarding this conversation,

00:49:00.494 --> 00:49:04.800
what I think is also makes
things very successful,

00:49:04.800 --> 00:49:06.660
like change very successful,

00:49:06.660 --> 00:49:09.776
is that both Asmaa and Rana for example,

00:49:09.776 --> 00:49:14.776
are giving a message that they are model.

00:49:16.320 --> 00:49:17.580
I mean they're modeling something

00:49:17.580 --> 00:49:19.200
that they do for themselves.

00:49:19.200 --> 00:49:21.120
So for example,

00:49:21.120 --> 00:49:22.890
Asmaa is talking to the girls

00:49:22.890 --> 00:49:26.040
about having a good
relationship with their father

00:49:26.040 --> 00:49:28.710
and she is going with her father

00:49:28.710 --> 00:49:30.690
and building that relationship.

00:49:30.690 --> 00:49:33.540
She's talking to the
girls about dreaming big

00:49:33.540 --> 00:49:36.810
and here she is giving conversations

00:49:36.810 --> 00:49:39.843
and in discussions and giving the message.

00:49:40.709 --> 00:49:43.710
She imagines herself outside of Jordan

00:49:43.710 --> 00:49:44.903
and here she is.

00:49:44.903 --> 00:49:47.341
Rana wanted to start a
We Love Reading program

00:49:47.341 --> 00:49:50.010
and she was there reading to children.

00:49:50.010 --> 00:49:55.010
So I think part of this
whole idea of creating change

00:49:55.380 --> 00:49:57.280
is also being the change that you want

00:49:58.400 --> 00:50:00.360
to bring to the world,

00:50:00.360 --> 00:50:04.140
and I think that's why they're
both so, so successful.

00:50:04.140 --> 00:50:05.900
- Everybody can be that I think...

00:50:05.900 --> 00:50:08.010
- Everybody can be, yeah.

00:50:08.010 --> 00:50:11.580
- It doesn't have to be
meaning we have to do it big.

00:50:11.580 --> 00:50:13.230
We always talk, it's
about the small steps.

00:50:13.230 --> 00:50:16.397
It's about doing the small
things and being persistent,

00:50:16.397 --> 00:50:18.651
which is the chaos
theory in physics, right?

00:50:18.651 --> 00:50:20.970
When a butterfly flutters its wings,

00:50:20.970 --> 00:50:22.560
it moves the air a centimeter

00:50:22.560 --> 00:50:24.540
but there's a huge
change in time and space

00:50:24.540 --> 00:50:25.860
beyond what we can imagine.

00:50:25.860 --> 00:50:28.110
And actually both me and Asmaa,

00:50:28.110 --> 00:50:31.320
we draw this from our religion
as Muslims, both of us.

00:50:31.320 --> 00:50:34.140
Where in Islam it tells
us be the guardian,

00:50:34.140 --> 00:50:35.490
so take care of your community

00:50:35.490 --> 00:50:37.500
and it says do not belittle any good deed.

00:50:37.500 --> 00:50:39.120
Even if it's small, do it.

00:50:39.120 --> 00:50:40.313
You're not responsible for the results.

00:50:40.313 --> 00:50:41.551
You're responsible to try.

00:50:41.551 --> 00:50:43.846
That's 'cause you can control trying,

00:50:43.846 --> 00:50:45.690
and that's all you need.

00:50:45.690 --> 00:50:49.800
And I think any person can, inside you,

00:50:49.800 --> 00:50:51.420
you know that if you really want something

00:50:51.420 --> 00:50:52.530
and you go and do it,

00:50:52.530 --> 00:50:54.153
things will happen eventually.

